    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that musical chord analogy makes sense. but what about the \"hidden\" stuff? like if i have a signal that looks totally messy and random in the time domain, is the fourier transform basically just uncovering all these secret frequencies that were hiding in there the whole time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, that's a great way to put it; it uncovers the hidden composition. A messy signal in the time domain might look like random noise, but the Fourier Transform can reveal underlying patterns, revealing specific, strong sinusoidal components even if they are mixed together. Those \"secret\" frequencies are the fundamental building blocks that, when added up, create that complex waveform you see over time.",
      "source": "target",
