    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "so it's basically like a filter or something? once you know what those hidden frequency components are, can you just pull them out or manipulate them? like if i wanted to remove a specific frequency from a noisy signal, how would that actually look in practice?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exactly, once you know the frequencies, you can manipulate them using filters in the frequency domain. To remove a specific frequency, you essentially create a \"notch filter\" in the frequency domain—a filter that zeros out the exact frequency component you want gone. After removing that unwanted frequency, you use the inverse Fourier Transform to switch the signal back into the time domain, resulting in a denoised output. This spectral manipulation is incredibly powerful for signal processing.",
      "source": "target",
